### Describe the bug

When an entity has a polymorphic embeddable (array of embeddables with `discriminatorColumn`) and two children declare a same-named nested embeddable whose relation properties differ (child A has `in.departureAirport`, child B has `in.originAirport`), loading the owner with `populate: ['*']` throws:

```ts
TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'name')
    at expandDotPaths (@mikro-orm/core/entity/utils.js:48:37)
    at EntityLoader.normalizePopulate (@mikro-orm/core/entity/EntityLoader.js:102:9)
    at EntityLoader.populate (@mikro-orm/core/entity/EntityLoader.js:43:25)
    at populateChildren (@mikro-orm/core/entity/EntityLoader.js:563:24)
    at async EntityLoader.populateField (@mikro-orm/core/entity/EntityLoader.js:598:13)
    at async EntityLoader.populate (@mikro-orm/core/entity/EntityLoader.js:59:13)
    at async MongoEntityManager.find (@mikro-orm/core/EntityManager.js:167:9)
```

Once such a child is registered, **every** `em.find()` with `populate: ['*']` on the owning entity throws, including rows that don't use that discriminator. We hit this in production on the MongoDB driver (a discriminated `data` embeddable with one shape per country).

**Root cause (verified by instrumenting the crash site).** Logging right before `utils.js:48` at the moment of the crash prints:

```
metaClass: 'DataA | DataB' // the polymorphic union metadata
hintField: 'in.originAirport' // hint derived from DataB
embeddedPropsKeys: ['departureAirport'] // the union's `in` only kept DataA's shape
```

`populate: ['*']` correctly derives hints from **all** polymorph targets (both `in.departureAirport` and `in.originAirport` are enumerated), but in the union metadata the same-named child `in` keeps the `embeddedProps` of only one branch (same-named children collapse when child props are merged by name). `populateChildren` (`EntityLoader.js:563`, polymorphic loop at `:584`) then resolves every hint against that collapsed metadata, and for the other branch's hint `Object.values(prop.embeddedProps).find(c => c.embedded[1] === parts[0])` finds nothing, so `embeddedProp.name` throws.

Two data points that may help with the fix:

- Simply guarding the failing lookups (skip unknown segments in `expandDotPaths` / `expandNestedPopulate`) stops the crash, but the sibling branch's relation then silently stays unpopulated (verified: `originAirport` remains an unhydrated FK while `departureAirport` loads fine). So the fix probably needs per-branch `embeddedProps` on the union metadata (or hints resolved against each polymorph target's own metadata) rather than defensive guards.
- Control experiment: renaming the property so both children share the same name (`departureAirport` in both) makes the exact same script load both rows fully populated.

### Reproduction

```ts
import { EntitySchema, MikroORM } from "@mikro-orm/mongodb";

class Airport {}

const airportEntity = new EntitySchema({
	class: Airport,
	properties: {
		_id: { primary: true, type: "ObjectId" },
		code: { type: "string" },
		id: { serializedPrimaryKey: true, type: "string" },
	},
});

const dataEntity = new EntitySchema({
	abstract: true,
	discriminatorColumn: "type",
	embeddable: true,
	name: "Data",
	properties: {
		type: { type: "string" },
	},
});

// child A: `in.departureAirport`
const aInEntity = new EntitySchema({
	embeddable: true,
	name: "AIn",
	properties: {
		departureAirport: { entity: () => airportEntity, kind: "m:1", ref: true },
	},
});

const dataAEntity = new EntitySchema({
	discriminatorValue: "a",
	embeddable: true,
	extends: dataEntity,
	name: "DataA",
	properties: {
		in: { entity: () => aInEntity, kind: "embedded", object: true },
	},
});

// child B: same child name `in`, DIFFERENT relation name `originAirport`
const bInEntity = new EntitySchema({
	embeddable: true,
	name: "BIn",
	properties: {
		originAirport: { entity: () => airportEntity, kind: "m:1", ref: true },
	},
});

const dataBEntity = new EntitySchema({
	discriminatorValue: "b",
	embeddable: true,
	extends: dataEntity,
	name: "DataB",
	properties: {
		in: { entity: () => bInEntity, kind: "embedded", object: true },
	},
});

class Doc {}

const docEntity = new EntitySchema({
	class: Doc,
	properties: {
		_id: { primary: true, type: "ObjectId" },
		data: {
			entity: () => [dataAEntity, dataBEntity],
			kind: "embedded",
			object: true,
		},
		id: { serializedPrimaryKey: true, type: "string" },
	},
});

