### Description

When using the class-less `defineEntity` overload (without `class` property), hook callbacks such as `beforeCreate` and `beforeUpdate` lose all type safety because `DefineEntityHooks` defaults its generic to `any`.

This means `EventArgs<any>` is the only option for external hook functions, losing type safety on `args.entity`, `args.changeSet?.payload`, etc.

### Reproduction

Minimal reproduction repository: https://github.com/winetree94/reproduction

```typescript
import { defineEntity, EventArgs, p } from '@mikro-orm/core';
import { hash } from 'argon2';

// ✅ works — but loses type safety
// async function hashPassword(args: EventArgs<any>) {
//   const password = args.changeSet?.payload.password;
//   if (typeof password === 'string') {
//     args.entity.password = await hash(password);
//   }
// }

// ❌ does not work — circular type reference
async function hashPassword(args: EventArgs<User>) {
  const password = args.changeSet?.payload.password;
  if (typeof password === 'string') {
    args.entity.password = await hash(password);
  }
}

export const UserSchema = defineEntity({
  name: 'User',
  properties: {
    fullName: p.string(),
    email: p.string(),
    password: p.string().hidden().lazy(),
    bio: p.text().default(''),
  },
  hooks: {
    beforeCreate: [hashPassword],
    beforeUpdate: [hashPassword],
  },
});

export class User extends UserSchema.class {}

UserSchema.setClass(User);
```

### Why This Happens

The entity type for the class-less overload is `InferEntityFromProperties<TProperties, ...>`, which is computed from the properties object. Since `hooks` is a property of the same object, referencing the inferred entity type in `hooks` creates a circular type dependency:

```
hooks (needs Entity type for EventArgs<Entity>)
  ↑
defineEntity config object
  ↑
InferEntityFromProperties (computes Entity type from config)
  ↑
hooks (part of config)  ← CIRCULAR
```

When using the `setClass()` pattern (define schema first, then create and link a class), the hook function cannot reference the class type either, since the class depends on the schema and the schema's hooks need the class type.

In `EntityMetadataWithProperties`, hooks are typed as:
```typescript
hooks?: DefineEntityHooks; // T defaults to any
```

So hook callbacks become `(args: EventArgs<any>) => void | Promise<void>`, and there is no way to get the inferred entity type in the hook function.

Note that the class-based overload (overload 2, with `class` property) **does** correctly type hooks as `DefineEntityHooks<TEntity>` since `TEntity` comes from the class independently.

### Expected Behavior

Hook callbacks should be type-safe even in the class-less overload. Some possible approaches:

1. **`addHooks` method**: A post-definition API where the entity type is already resolved:
   ```typescript
   const schema = defineEntity({
     name: 'User',
     properties: { ... },
   });

   schema.addHooks({
     beforeCreate: [(args) => { /* args.entity is typed */ }],
   });
   ```

2. **`defineHook` helper**: A utility function that allows defining typed hooks separately:
   ```typescript
   const hashPassword = defineHook<typeof UserSchema>((args) => {
     args.entity.password; // ✅ type-safe
   });
   ```

3. **Builder pattern**: Chain hooks after entity definition:
   ```typescript
   const UserSchema = defineEntity({ ... })
     .hooks({
       beforeCreate: [(args) => { /* typed */ }],
     });
   ```

